WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.416 --> 00:00:02.887
- It's so nice to see you in person.

00:00:02.887 --> 00:00:03.720
- It's so nice to see you.

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:05.161
- 'Cause you were virtual
last time I saw you.

00:00:05.161 --> 00:00:06.300
- Yes, yes, yes.
- I thought, yes, yes, yes.

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.040
I'm very excited you're here.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:09.170
Look at your energy.
- I'm very excited to be here.

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:10.003
Well, come on.

00:00:10.003 --> 00:00:12.390
- Is that caffeine or natural?
- No.

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:14.530
- I was like, "What are you doing?"

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:16.401
- Listen, they wind me up and out I go.

00:00:16.401 --> 00:00:18.670
- And side me out, like a bunny.

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:21.330
Wait, so happy Mother's
Day and RAD mom week.

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:23.630
So wait, do y'all do
anything special and your son

00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:24.500
for Mother's Day?

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:27.310
- Do you know what, he usually writes me,

00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:29.340
I make him write me something.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:31.620
And last year was the greatest one.

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:35.640
He had this jam jar that he
filled with all these messages

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:38.713
of things that he thought about me.

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:40.603
- [Kelly] What?

00:00:40.603 --> 00:00:44.588
- My favorite was, "Sometimes
your hair looks nice."

00:00:44.588 --> 00:00:45.760
(audience laughs)

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:47.047
- My kids would be like,

00:00:47.047 --> 00:00:49.526
"Sometimes your hair isn't in a bun."

00:00:49.526 --> 00:00:50.730
- Exactly.

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:52.240
- So, wait, you posted this photo

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:56.250
and it's you and your son
younger and this is awesome.

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:57.442
- [Minnie] Yeah.

00:00:57.442 --> 00:00:58.275
(audience applauds)

00:00:58.275 --> 00:01:02.170
- First of all, he has such
rad rock and roll hair.

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:04.110
Like, I mean, is that, are you happy

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:04.943
'cause he got your hair?

00:01:04.943 --> 00:01:06.500
- Yeah, he really did.
- I love it.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:09.040
- It's crazy and he--
- But it's cool.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:10.937
It's really rock and roll.
- It's cool, it's good hair.

00:01:10.937 --> 00:01:13.140
And he's in two bands right now.

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:15.930
He's in a kind of jazz
band and a rock band.

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:17.940
- A jazz band?
- [Minnie] I know.

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:18.773
- Wow!

00:01:18.773 --> 00:01:21.460
- And the hair is, the
hair is great on stage.

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:23.360
- Yeah. That's what I'm saying.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:24.603
He was destined to be
on stage with that hair.

00:01:24.603 --> 00:01:26.710
- I think he probably was.

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:28.250
- So I find this interesting too.

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:30.620
So you, this wasn't
your idea, this was his.

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:32.120
He wanted to go to boarding school.

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:33.450
- Well, so here's the thing.

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:34.930
I would never in a million years

00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:36.870
have let my child go to boarding school

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:39.540
'cause all I'm like his worst groupie.

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:42.229
Like all I wanna do is be
around him all the time.

00:01:42.229 --> 00:01:43.240
- 24/7.
- You wanna hang out?

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:44.073
But during COVID--

00:01:44.073 --> 00:01:45.529
- Is this why he wanted
to go to boarding school?

00:01:45.529 --> 00:01:46.724
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:46.724 --> 00:01:49.720
- Now Kelly, yes probably.

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:51.529
We went in during COVID.

00:01:51.529 --> 00:01:54.050
The school stayed shut in Los Angeles

00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:56.840
but they opened in that September of 2020.

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:57.940
They reopened in England.

00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:00.350
And my son said, "Please
can I go to a real school?"

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:02.420
And we only meant to go for a semester.

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:06.550
And during that time he
reconnected with his British roots

00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:10.410
and fell in love so hard
with the slate grey sky,

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:12.840
the horizontal rain, the freezing cold,

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:14.600
the pitch black by 3:00 p.m.

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:16.440
and was like, "This is my place."

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:18.070
And I was like, "Are you sure?

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:19.140
Like you were raised

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:20.580
at the beach."
- He's in a band.

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:21.990
Like he's artistic, he's into it.

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:22.823
- [Minnie] He's fully--

00:02:22.823 --> 00:02:24.960
- I love England for
those reasons, by the way.

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:25.930
- Seriously?

00:02:25.930 --> 00:02:26.763
- Love it.

00:02:26.763 --> 00:02:27.910
I was born on a rainy day.

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:30.040
I was born in like
literally it was storming.

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:31.280
My brother will remind everyone

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:33.768
because he got pulled
from his game, sorry.

00:02:33.768 --> 00:02:35.700
Like some basketball game.

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:37.320
I'm like, "You aren't gonna
be a basketball player.

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:38.397
It's fine."

00:02:38.397 --> 00:02:39.230
Like it's like, but anyway.

00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:41.762
We are here with Minnie
Driver, give it up.

00:02:41.762 --> 00:02:43.660
(audience applauds)

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:48.100
Whose new book is called
"Managing Expectations."

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:50.430
I love this title.

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:51.770
I always have a joke with my friends.

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:52.990
I'm like, "That's what
I'm gonna name my book."

00:02:52.990 --> 00:02:55.010
I say, I change the name all the time

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:56.910
but you always thought
you'd name a book this

00:02:56.910 --> 00:02:57.780
if you wrote a book.

00:02:57.780 --> 00:02:59.050
Why that?

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:00.400
Did you have a hard time managing them?

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:01.233
- Yeah, I mean,

00:03:01.233 --> 00:03:02.430
I think we, don't we all?

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:05.210
Like the expectations
that we have of ourselves,

00:03:05.210 --> 00:03:07.140
that our parents have
of us, that society does

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:10.670
as women, parents, lovers, friends,

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:13.390
everything seems to be about
managing these expectations.

00:03:13.390 --> 00:03:16.670
And then the big sort of spiritual nugget

00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:17.980
that you finally realize

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:21.250
is that expectations
is really the problem.

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:25.320
And that if we got rid of that
idea of expecting something

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:26.890
but rather lived in this moment

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:28.110
and allowed it to be something.

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:31.970
So the title was ironic
and, but we all do it.

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:33.590
And the book is so much about

00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:35.900
how stuff in your life not working out

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:39.210
is really your life
working out in other ways.

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:42.210
- Yeah. I love that.
- I don't know.

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:43.043
It's tricky but--

00:03:43.043 --> 00:03:44.020
- Everybody has a hard time I feel like

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:46.520
with managing expectations
more so for ourselves

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:48.540
that we set for ourselves
than even others.

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:50.240
- Yeah, definitely, definitely.

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:51.870
Or if you've sort of have in your head

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:54.110
what you perceive someone's
expectation of you

00:03:54.110 --> 00:03:56.520
to be like it's all created

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:59.710
and it's all already setting
you at a disadvantage

00:03:59.710 --> 00:04:03.330
as opposed to stay in the
moment, live your life

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:06.650
and don't judge it or
expect it to look different.

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:07.775
Just be in it.

00:04:07.775 --> 00:04:08.980
Which is by the way,
- It seems more free.

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:12.060
- When somebody dies, who you
love, as anyone who knows.

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:15.220
It's like it's done and it is done fast.

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:17.150
This is, well it is over in an instant.

00:04:17.150 --> 00:04:18.630
Like there's no time to waste

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:20.970
with thinking it should look differently.

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:24.607
Let it be what it is and
enjoy every bloody second.

00:04:24.607 --> 00:04:27.973
(audience applauds)

00:04:27.973 --> 00:04:31.250
- Well one story in the book
is how dancing at raves,

00:04:31.250 --> 00:04:32.760
which I love that you
went dancing at raves

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:34.250
got you into acting.

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:35.550
What?
- Oh my God!

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:38.300
- Well, so I graduated from drama school.

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:40.690
You know, this conservatory,
I went to study acting

00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:42.850
and I was the only kid who
didn't have representation

00:04:42.850 --> 00:04:44.950
at the end when everyone graduated.

00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:48.600
And that summer I graduated
in May and that summer,

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:51.670
it was the explosion of
house music and acid house.

00:04:51.670 --> 00:04:54.430
And these raves were popping
up all over the place.

00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:56.534
And I just used to go to dance.

00:04:56.534 --> 00:04:58.800
- I love dancing.
- To forget my worries.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:04:59.633
I was like,
- Escapism.

00:04:59.633 --> 00:05:02.050
- I have no idea what my life is gonna be

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:04.600
now that apparently I'm
not gonna be an actor.

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:06.710
So I used to go dance.

00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:08.760
And because I didn't do all the substances

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:10.300
that all the other kids were doing

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:13.410
because I was square and I didn't really--

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:14.790
- Or terrified 'cause a lot of them

00:05:14.790 --> 00:05:16.120
causes holes in the brain.

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:17.240
- I didn't really know what the,

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:18.073
I didn't really.
- Me neither.

00:05:18.073 --> 00:05:21.000
- Anyway, I just, I was the
sober driver coming home.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:23.870
So my car would be full
of crazy casualties.

00:05:23.870 --> 00:05:25.860
And there was this other kid.

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:27.400
- Crazy casualties
should be your next book.

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:28.743
- By the way, next book.

00:05:28.743 --> 00:05:30.129
(Kelly and Minnie laugh)

00:05:30.129 --> 00:05:31.023
- Crazy casualties?

00:05:31.023 --> 00:05:31.856
(audience clapping)

00:05:31.856 --> 00:05:33.980
Am I gonna steal that for a song?

00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:36.150
Wait, crazy casualties is brilliant.

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:37.400
- We'll release the book with,
- With a song.

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:40.034
- It'll come with a song
with it to download.

00:05:40.034 --> 00:05:41.590
But anyway, there was this other kid

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:44.850
who was also sober and we
would drive home together.

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:46.730
And just through the
summer we were just chat

00:05:46.730 --> 00:05:48.570
and we chatted about boys and music.

00:05:48.570 --> 00:05:51.420
We both loved Nana Cherry
and Bell Biv DeVoe.

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:54.520
And we loved all the same kind of music.

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:55.800
And throughout the summer,

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:57.900
we never talked about life or anything.

00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:00.770
And at the end of the summer,
it was about to be September

00:06:00.770 --> 00:06:03.650
and going back to work and
the dread was building.

00:06:03.650 --> 00:06:05.170
And one day she was like,
"You know, what do you do?"

00:06:05.170 --> 00:06:06.890
And I was like, "Well, you know,

00:06:06.890 --> 00:06:08.230
I'm supposed to be an actress,

00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:09.640
but I mean, that's not really happening."

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:11.200
And she's like, "Oh, that's weird.

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:13.120
I work for a casting director."

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:15.197
And I was like, "Do you?"

00:06:16.738 --> 00:06:18.210
- That's so interesting.

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:20.450
- That's so nice that you do that.

00:06:20.450 --> 00:06:23.040
And she was like, "Yeah, don't
ask me to introduce you."

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:24.210
And I was like, "Okay."

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:25.870
And she was like, "Just kidding, go on."

00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:26.810
- [Kelly] Oh my God!

00:06:26.810 --> 00:06:28.300
- She was like, "You
should come and meet her."

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:29.197
- What is the happenstance of that?

00:06:29.197 --> 00:06:32.410
- And that Monday I went in
and this casting director,

00:06:32.410 --> 00:06:36.150
I had nothing to recommend me.

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:37.280
Like, no nothing.

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:40.347
She just had a cup of
tea with me and was like,

00:06:40.347 --> 00:06:42.260
"Yeah, you seem funny.

00:06:42.260 --> 00:06:43.710
I'll call someone."

00:06:43.710 --> 00:06:45.070
- [Kelly] Oh my gosh!

00:06:45.070 --> 00:06:47.002
- And she called and I got an agent, yeah.

00:06:47.002 --> 00:06:48.225
(audience applauds)

00:06:48.225 --> 00:06:49.350
- What?
- It was crazy, it's crazy.

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:50.780
- Never give up and go to raves

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:51.970
is what we've learned here today.

00:06:51.970 --> 00:06:54.540
- Yeah. Oh! Don't do drugs an dungs.

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:55.920
- Don't do drugs and dungs.

00:06:55.920 --> 00:06:57.080
I like the use of dungs.

00:06:57.080 --> 00:07:00.497
So wait, so last time we
talked about surfing in Malibu.

00:07:00.497 --> 00:07:03.730
So were you there when
the fires got crazy?

00:07:03.730 --> 00:07:04.760
- Oh my gosh! Yeah.

00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:07.060
I was working and my,

00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:10.300
I watched on the news as the
fire was barreling down the PCH

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:13.610
and it was headed straight
for the little tiny community

00:07:13.610 --> 00:07:14.580
where I live.
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:18.530
- And because of the
bravery of 10 men who stayed

00:07:18.530 --> 00:07:21.510
and fought the fire, a
couple of ex firefighters

00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:25.130
and some lifeguards,
they saved our community

00:07:25.130 --> 00:07:26.980
but my community was completely cut off.

00:07:26.980 --> 00:07:28.970
They had one FEMA meal a day

00:07:28.970 --> 00:07:31.150
but they were running out of
gasoline for the generators

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:32.380
'cause there was no power.

00:07:32.380 --> 00:07:34.670
They were running out
of eyewash, chocolate,

00:07:34.670 --> 00:07:36.750
booze, everything.

00:07:36.750 --> 00:07:38.840
- All the key things.
- All important things.

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:39.752
- All of the vital
components for survival.

00:07:39.752 --> 00:07:42.623
- When things are heating
the fan you need booze.

00:07:42.623 --> 00:07:44.110
- So I was like, "Okay."

00:07:44.110 --> 00:07:46.680
I'd just gone through a terrible breakup

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:49.410
and was maybe not in
the best frame of mind

00:07:49.410 --> 00:07:50.337
but I was like,

00:07:50.337 --> 00:07:52.850
"I need to deliver goods."
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:07:52.850 --> 00:07:55.120
- However, the only way you
could get in was by boat

00:07:55.120 --> 00:07:57.390
and because people had started looting,

00:07:57.390 --> 00:08:00.550
the Coast Guard had issued
a zero ship to shore.

00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:02.470
So you'd be arrested
if you tried to get in.

00:08:02.470 --> 00:08:05.740
Nonetheless, I found a boat and a captain

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:07.010
and I just had to figure out

00:08:07.010 --> 00:08:09.820
how I was gonna get the stuff
from the boat into the beach

00:08:09.820 --> 00:08:11.250
and I needed some help.

00:08:11.250 --> 00:08:12.680
- You became a good pirate.

00:08:12.680 --> 00:08:14.021
- I did.
- I like it.

00:08:14.021 --> 00:08:15.727
- And I called, I was like,

00:08:15.727 --> 00:08:17.760
"Who can I get to go do this with me?"

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:19.670
So I remembered this dude that I'd met

00:08:19.670 --> 00:08:21.030
at a friend's breakfast.

00:08:21.030 --> 00:08:22.150
And he was just this really

00:08:22.150 --> 00:08:24.630
Intrepid Indian Jones type explorer.

00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:25.860
- Perfect guy.

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:27.010
- Called him up.

00:08:27.010 --> 00:08:31.340
I was like, "Will you help me
launch a sea-based incursion?

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:35.120
You might get arrested."
- A sea based incursion?

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:36.410
- There was a big silence

00:08:36.410 --> 00:08:37.700
and then he was like, "Yeah, I'll do it."

00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:39.430
- [Kelly] You made him arrested?

00:08:39.430 --> 00:08:40.780
- And I, we did it.

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:42.180
We got chased by the Coast Guard.

00:08:42.180 --> 00:08:45.530
We got, you know, there is
a mounted gun on that boat.

00:08:45.530 --> 00:08:49.240
Like we had the gasoline
balanced on the paddle boards,

00:08:49.240 --> 00:08:51.830
we were paddling it in, we
dragged it on the beach.

00:08:51.830 --> 00:08:53.390
We ran away from,

00:08:53.390 --> 00:08:55.630
I mean, the Coast Guard hung out all day

00:08:55.630 --> 00:08:57.820
waiting for us to come back.

00:08:57.820 --> 00:09:00.290
We were shipwrecked in my
house that had no electricity,

00:09:00.290 --> 00:09:01.350
no water, nothing.

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:03.910
And he basically, I
asked him every question

00:09:03.910 --> 00:09:06.110
about why men are unfaithful,

00:09:06.110 --> 00:09:07.762
why men do the things that they do.

00:09:07.762 --> 00:09:08.774
(Kelly laughs)

00:09:08.774 --> 00:09:11.000
I was like, "You are
trapped, I'm really sorry.

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:12.530
I wanna ask you a lot of questions."

00:09:12.530 --> 00:09:16.410
And he was kind of
like, "Well, all right."

00:09:16.410 --> 00:09:17.243
- [Kelly] Oh my God.

00:09:17.243 --> 00:09:19.370
- And so we hung out the whole day.

00:09:19.370 --> 00:09:20.790
I got to know him.

00:09:20.790 --> 00:09:23.120
We made it back to the Marina del Rey

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:24.540
at like, you know, midnight.

00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:26.160
We're standing on the thing.

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:27.500
I was like, "I'm ruined.

00:09:27.500 --> 00:09:28.960
I'm never gonna have another boyfriend."

00:09:28.960 --> 00:09:30.620
